window.AG_CHAPTERS["13"] = {"id":"13","title":"The Dyer's Failing Sight","titleZh":"染坊師傅的眼睛","patient":{"name":"Master Yan 顏師傅","sprite":"man"},"scene":{"img":"art/scene-hall.jpg","key":"rain"},"pattern":"Liver and Kidney Yin Deficiency 肝腎陰虛","principle":"nourish the yin of the Liver and the Kidney together","tongue":"red body, little coating","pulseNote":{"en":"Fine as a thread and quick with it — narrow, and hurrying.","zh":"細如絲而數，脈道不充而其來也急"},"needleAsk":{"en":"Needle <b>T&#224;ix&#299; KI3</b>. Tap its exact location.","zh":"行針 — 點出穴位精確位置"},"target":{"id":"KI3","zh":"太谿","py":"T&#224;ix&#299;","x":113,"y":868,"img":"art/body-leg-medial.jpg","w":434,"h":1159,"view":{"x":40,"y":660,"w":300,"h":420},"decoys":[{"x":178,"y":722},{"x":196,"y":900},{"x":150,"y":876},{"x":88,"y":762},{"x":232,"y":872}],"note":"<b>太谿 T&#224;ix&#299; KI3</b> — in the depression between the tip of the medial malleolus and the Achilles tendon, level with the tip of the malleolus. Source point of the Kidney; it serves Kidney yin and Kidney yang alike.<br>內踝尖與跟腱之間凹陷處，與內踝尖平齊。腎之原穴，陰陽俱調。"},"hints":{"ask":{"en":"Two organs, and each opens into a different sense. Ask about the <b>eyes</b>, and ask about the <b>ears</b>.","zh":"病涉二臟，各開一竅。當問其目，當問其耳。"},"look":{"en":"Red with almost no coating. Ask yourself what grows a tongue coating, and what its absence means.","zh":"舌紅而幾無苔。當自問：苔何以生？其無苔又何謂？"},"pulse":{"en":"Narrow because there is not enough to fill it; quick because heat has been let loose.","zh":"細者不足以充，數者熱失其制。"},"dx":{"en":"Is the emptiness in yin or in yang? Then find the evidence for each organ separately. Then say why these two fail together.","zh":"所虛在陰在陽？次求二臟各自之憑。末言二者何以同病。"},"rx":{"en":"Nourish water so that wood is watered. And one point on the leg reaches three yin channels at once.","zh":"滋水以涵木。且有一穴在脛，一針而通三陰。"},"tech":{"en":"You needled this same point in the ferryman's winter. The technique is not the same. Ask why.","zh":"吳伯之冬，你曾針此同一穴。手法則異。當自問其故。"}},"reactions":[{"who":"master","en":"Master Shen notices him reading the wall scroll at arm's length, then closer, then further.","zh":"參師父見他看牆上字幅，先遠一臂，又近，又遠。"},{"who":"patient","en":"Master Yan rubs his eyes with the back of his wrist, out of long habit.","zh":"顏師傅以腕背揉目，積習已久。"},{"who":"boy","en":"The herb boy dims the lamp without being asked. Master Yan looks relieved.","zh":"藥童不待吩咐就把燈調暗。顏師傅神色一鬆。"},{"who":"master","en":"\"Forty years matching dye lots by eye. Now he cannot tell indigo from black after dusk.\"","zh":"「四十年以目辨色。如今入夜，靛青與玄黑已分不清。」"},{"who":"patient","en":"\"There's a ringing too,\" he says. \"High, both sides. Worse when I'm tired.\"","zh":"「還有耳鳴，」他說，「兩耳皆有，聲高，倦時尤甚。」"},{"who":"master","en":"\"Eyes and ears together, in a man of sixty. Two organs, and they fail as a pair for a reason.\"","zh":"「目與耳並見，其人六十。病涉二臟，而二者同衰，自有其故。」"}],"remedy":{"ask":"cold_heat","look":"cold_heat","pulse":"cold_heat","dx":"excess_def","rx":"channel_organ","tech":"cold_heat"},"reward":{"cardEn":"Water Nourishes Wood","cardZh":"滋水涵木 · 乙癸同源","cardTxt":"Liver blood and Kidney essence come from the same source and turn into one another, which is why they run down together. Blurred vision and tinnitus arriving in the same patient is the classic pair. Nourish the water and the wood is watered — treating the Liver alone leaves the well dry.","herbId":"gouqi","herbEn":"Goji Berry","herbZh":"枸杞子","herbImg":"art/herb-gouqi.jpg","sprite":"art/sprite-renshen-jump.webp","herbTxt":"Sweet and neutral, entering Liver, Kidney and Lung. It nourishes Liver blood and Kidney yin and brightens the eyes — one of the few kitchen-familiar herbs that addresses exactly this pair. Gentle enough to be taken in soup and tea, which suits a pattern that takes months to turn."},"endings":{"three":["At the spring dyeing Master Yan matches the indigo lots himself. \"The ringing is still there,\" he says. \"But quieter, and I can see the difference in the vats again.\"<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"You treated the water, not only the wood. A man's eyes are fed from a well two organs deep — most students never look past the eye.\"","春染之時，顏師傅親自對靛。「耳鳴還在，」他說，「但輕了，缸裡的色差我又看得出來了。」<br><b>參師父：</b>「你治其水，不獨治其木。目之所養，其井深在二臟。多數學生看不出眼睛以外。」"],"two":["His eyes are steadier in daylight. The ringing is unchanged and he still wakes at three.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"You nourished the wood. The well it draws from is still low.\"","白日視物穩了些，耳鳴如故，仍是三更即醒。<br><b>參師父：</b>「你養其木。其所汲之井，水仍未足。」"],"one":["No change. On the way out he asks the herb boy to read a label for him.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"Eyes and ears. You treated one of them. They come from the same well.\"","未見好轉。臨去時，他請藥童替他讀了一個藥籤。<br><b>參師父：</b>「目與耳。你只治了其一。二者同汲一井。」"],"titles":["Master Yan Matches the Indigo","Master Yan Sees Better by Day","Master Yan Will Need Another Visit"]},"script":[{"w":"master","en":"You have seen two organs press on each other, and two organs feed each other. Now two that simply run down together.","cn":"你已見二臟相迫，又見二臟相養。今則見二臟同衰。"},{"w":"patient","en":"Doctor. Yan, master dyer. My eyes are going. Blurred by evening, and dry all day.","cn":"大夫。姓顏，染坊師傅。目力漸衰，入夜則糊，終日乾澀。"},{"w":"patient","en":"My low back aches, my knees are weak on the stairs, and there is a ringing in my ears that never stops.","cn":"腰痠，登階則膝軟，耳中鳴響不絕。"},{"w":"master","en":"The Liver opens into the eyes. The Kidney opens into the ears. He has just named both organs without knowing it.","cn":"肝開竅於目，腎開竅於耳。其人不自知，而二臟已自其口而出。"},{"w":"master","en":"Now find out whether what is missing is warmth, or substance. The two look nothing alike once you ask properly.","cn":"今當辨其所失者，溫乎？形乎？問得其法，則二者迥然可別。"}],"qa":[{"q":"Describe the eyes — dry, blurred, or both?","cq":"目之所苦：乾？糊？抑或兼之？","good":1,"a":"Both. Dry and gritty all day, and by evening the edges of things go soft.","ca":"兼之。終日乾澀如沙，入夜則物之邊際模糊。","clue":{"en":"Dry, gritty, blurred vision worse in the evening","zh":"目乾澀、視糊、入夜尤甚"},"note":"The Liver stores blood and opens into the eyes; when Liver blood and yin are short the eyes are not moistened or nourished. Worse in the evening fits, because yin is what should be rising as the day ends. 肝藏血，開竅於目。肝之血與陰不足，則目失濡養。入夜尤甚者，日暮本為陰所當升之時。"},{"q":"Tell me about the ringing — one ear or both, high or low?","cq":"耳鳴：一耳抑或兩耳？其聲高低？","good":1,"a":"Both, high and thin, and worse when I am tired. It has come on gradually over years.","ca":"兩耳皆鳴，聲高而細，倦則加重。積年漸來。","clue":{"en":"Bilateral high thin tinnitus, gradual, worse with fatigue","zh":"兩耳鳴，聲細而高，漸起，勞則甚"},"note":"<b>Blurred vision plus tinnitus in one patient is the classic lock for this pattern.</b> And the character of the tinnitus separates deficiency from excess: gradual, high, thin, worse with fatigue is empty; sudden, loud, low, worse with anger is Liver fire. 視糊兼耳鳴，本證之定式。且耳鳴之質可辨虛實：漸起、聲細高、勞則甚者為虛；驟起、聲大而低、怒則甚者為肝火。"},{"q":"Do you get hot in the afternoons, or sweat at night?","cq":"午後潮熱嗎？夜間盜汗嗎？","good":1,"a":"Yes to both, and my palms and soles burn. I sleep badly and dream a great deal.","ca":"二者皆有，且手足心熱。睡不安，多夢。","clue":{"en":"Afternoon heat, night sweats, five-centre heat, restless dreaming sleep","zh":"午後潮熱、盜汗、五心煩熱、寐差多夢"},"note":"This is empty heat — heat that appears because there is too little yin to hold yang down, not because a hot pathogen arrived. Afternoon timing, night sweats and five-centre heat 五心煩熱 are its signature, and they are what make this yin deficiency rather than blood deficiency. 此為虛熱：陰不足以制陽，非外邪之熱。午後而作、盜汗、五心煩熱，其徵也。有此三者，方為陰虛而非血虛。"},{"q":"Are your hands and feet cold, and is your urine clear and copious?","cq":"手足冷嗎？小便清長嗎？","good":0,"a":"The opposite — my palms burn, and my urine is scant and dark.","ca":"適反：手心發熱，小便短而色深。","clue":"","note":"A decisive exclusion. Cold limbs and clear copious urine would be yang deficiency — the ferryman of chapter ten, at the same point on the same channel. Burning palms and scant dark urine are its mirror image. Same organ, opposite half. 此問一決生死。肢冷、小便清長者，陽虛也，即第十章之吳伯，同經同穴。手心熱、尿短赤，適為其鏡像。同臟而異其半。"},{"q":"Do you belch or feel nauseated after eating?","cq":"食後噯氣或噁心嗎？","good":0,"a":"No. My digestion is the one thing still working properly.","ca":"沒有。飲食倒是唯一還好的。","clue":"","note":"A reasonable exclusion of the Stomach, and negative. It also rules out the Liver-invading-Stomach pattern, which would have brought belching and acid. 排除胃證，其結果為陰性。亦可排除肝胃不和之證：彼當見噯氣吞酸。"}],"look":[{"t":"Red body with little coating","c":"舌紅少苔","ok":1,"clue":{"en":"Red tongue, scant coat","zh":"舌紅少苔"},"note":"Correct, and the missing coating is the more important half. The coating is grown by stomach qi steaming fluids upward — so when fluids run low the coat thins and goes. Red says heat; scant coat says the heat is <i>empty</i>, born of shortage rather than invasion. 是，而無苔者尤要。苔由胃氣蒸津上潮而生，津虧則苔薄而剝。紅者主熱，少苔者言其熱為虛，生於不足而非外犯。"},{"t":"Pale swollen body, moist, with a white coat","c":"舌淡胖而潤、苔白","ok":0,"note":"Yang deficiency — chapter ten, the same organ, the opposite half. Pale versus red is the whole difference. 陽虛之象，即第十章，同臟而反其半。淡與紅，別即在此。"},{"t":"Pale body with a thin white coat","c":"舌淡苔薄白","ok":0,"note":"Blood deficiency. Close, and the eyes would suffer too — but blood deficiency brings no heat, no night sweats and no red tongue. 血虛之象。相近，其目亦當受累；然血虛無熱、無盜汗、舌亦不紅。"},{"t":"Red body with a thick yellow coat","c":"舌紅、苔黃厚","ok":0,"note":"Full heat with substance behind it. A thick coat means there is plenty of fluid to grow one — the opposite of this case. 實熱有形之象。苔厚者津足以生苔，與本案適反。"}],"pulse":[{"t":"Thready and rapid 細數","ok":1,"clue":{"en":"Thready rapid pulse","zh":"脈細數"},"note":"Correct, and it is the pulse of yin deficiency in two syllables. <b>Thready</b>: the vessel is under-filled, because yin and blood are what fill it. <b>Rapid</b>: heat — but heat without force, because there is no pathogen driving it. Thready-and-rapid together is empty heat; forceful-and-rapid would be full heat. 是，陰虛之脈，二字盡之。細者脈道不充，蓋充之者陰血也；數者熱，然其熱無力，以無邪驅之故。細數者虛熱，有力而數者實熱。"},{"t":"Deep, slow and weak 沉遲無力","ok":0,"note":"Yang deficiency, chapter ten. Slow versus rapid is the axis that separates the two halves of the same organ. 陽虛之脈，即第十章。遲與數，正分同臟之二半。"},{"t":"Wiry and rapid 弦數","ok":0,"note":"Liver fire — full heat rising. That patient is red-faced, angry and bitter-mouthed, with a forceful pulse. This one is thin and quiet. 肝火之脈，實熱上炎。其人面赤、易怒、口苦而脈有力。本案細而靜。"},{"t":"Slippery 滑","ok":0,"note":"Something substantial rolling in the vessel. This vessel is under-filled — the opposite finding. 脈中有形之物往來。本案脈道不充，適與之反。"}],"dx":[{"ask":"Step 1 — is the emptiness in yin or in yang?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第一步：所虛在陰，抑或在陽？</small>","opts":[{"t":"Yin — because heat came with the emptiness","c":"陰虛 — 虛而生熱","ok":1,"note":"Correct, and one question settles it: is he hot or cold? Yang deficiency is cold — chilled limbs, clear copious urine, a pale wet tongue, a slow pulse. Yin deficiency is hot — afternoon heat, night sweats, burning palms, scant dark urine, a red tongue with no coat, a rapid pulse. He has the second list entire, and he told you so when you asked about his hands. 是，一問可決：其人熱乎寒乎？陽虛者寒：肢冷、小便清長、舌淡潤、脈遲。陰虛者熱：午後潮熱、盜汗、手足心熱、尿短赤、舌紅無苔、脈數。本案全屬後者，問其手時已自言之。"},{"t":"Yang — the warming function has failed","c":"陽虛 — 溫煦失職","ok":0,"note":"Then he would be cold, and he is the opposite. This is the same organ as chapter ten and the opposite half of it — the single most examinable pair in the whole system. 若然，其人當寒，而適反。此與第十章同臟而反其半，為全系之中最常考之對。"},{"t":"Qi — the moving function has failed","c":"氣虛 — 推動失職","ok":0,"note":"Qi deficiency brings fatigue, breathlessness and daytime sweating with no temperature to it. There is no heat in a pure qi deficiency, and this case is full of it. 氣虛者倦怠、氣短、自汗，而無寒熱之偏。純氣虛無熱，本案熱象顯然。"}]},{"ask":"Step 2 — what is the evidence for each organ, separately?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第二步：二臟各自之憑為何？</small>","opts":[{"t":"Liver: dry blurred eyes. Kidney: tinnitus, sore low back, weak knees","c":"肝：目乾視糊。腎：耳鳴、腰痠、膝軟","ok":1,"note":"Correct, and it is worth learning as a pair of lists. <b>Liver</b> opens into the eyes, stores blood, governs the sinews and shows in the nails. <b>Kidney</b> opens into the ears, stores essence, governs the bones and the low back, and shows in the hair. Blurred vision plus tinnitus is the examinable lock for this pattern precisely because it names both orifices at once. 是，此二列宜並記。肝開竅於目、藏血、主筋、其華在爪；腎開竅於耳、藏精、主骨與腰、其華在髮。視糊兼耳鳴之所以為本證之鎖，正以其一舉而及二竅。"},{"t":"Liver: tinnitus. Kidney: blurred eyes","c":"肝：耳鳴。腎：目糊","ok":0,"note":"Reversed. The Liver opens into the eyes and the Kidney into the ears — swapping them is a standard wrong option. 顛倒矣。肝竅在目，腎竅在耳。互易者，考題之常設誤答。"},{"t":"Both sets of symptoms belong to the Kidney alone","c":"諸症皆屬腎","ok":0,"note":"The Kidney does not open into the eyes. And if it were one organ, the treatment would not need to reach two. 腎不開竅於目。且若止一臟，則治法不必及於二。"}]},{"ask":"Step 3 — why do these two organs fail together?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第三步：二臟何以同衰？</small>","opts":[{"t":"Liver blood and Kidney essence share one source and turn into one another","c":"乙癸同源 — 肝血腎精，互資互化","ok":1,"note":"<b>The idea the chapter exists to teach.</b> Classically 乙癸同源: the Liver (yi, wood) and the Kidney (gui, water) draw on one reserve — essence becomes blood and blood replenishes essence. So they run down as a pair, and they must be filled as a pair. In five-element terms, water nourishes wood 滋水涵木: fill the Kidney and the Liver is watered from below. Treating the eyes alone leaves the well dry. 本章所欲授者。古謂乙癸同源：肝木腎水，共汲一源，精化為血，血充其精。故同衰，亦當同補。以五行言之，滋水涵木：補其腎則肝自受其潤。獨治其目，井仍無水。"},{"t":"They are an interior-exterior pair","c":"二者相表裡","ok":0,"note":"They are not. Interior-exterior pairs are Liver-Gallbladder and Kidney-Bladder — a zang with its fu. Liver and Kidney are two zang. 非也。表裡者臟與其腑：肝配膽，腎配膀胱。肝腎皆臟。"},{"t":"The Liver controls the Kidney","c":"肝剋腎","ok":0,"note":"Wood does not control Water — Water generates Wood. Get the ring right: this is the generating cycle, not the controlling one. 木不剋水，水生木也。當辨其環：此相生而非相剋。"}]}],"rx":[{"id":"KI3","t":"T&#224;ix&#299; KI3 太谿","ok":1,"note":"Source point of the Kidney, and the point that fills the well. Uniquely it serves Kidney yin and Kidney yang alike, because a Source point restores whatever the organ is short of — which is why the same point appeared in chapter ten for the opposite pattern. 腎之原穴，所以實其井者。原穴補其所虧，故陰虛陽虛皆可取之——第十章反證亦用此穴，其理在此。"},{"id":"LR3","t":"T&#224;ich&#333;ng LR3 太衝","ok":1,"note":"Source point of the Liver. Here it is not soothing constraint but nourishing the organ itself — the same point doing a different job because the pattern is deficient rather than stuck. Paired with KI3 it treats both organs at their source. 肝之原穴。於此非疏其鬱，而養其臟——穴同而事異，以其證虛而非滯。與太谿相配，二臟俱從其原而治。"},{"id":"SP6","t":"S&#257;ny&#299;nji&#257;o SP6 三陰交","ok":1,"note":"Where the Spleen, Liver and Kidney channels cross — one needle reaching two of the three organs at fault, and the third makes the blood that replenishes them. It belongs in almost every yin-nourishing prescription for that reason. 足三陰交會之處：一針而及所病之二臟，其三則生血以資之。滋陰諸方多用之，正以此故。"},{"id":"LI4","t":"H&#233;g&#468; LI4 合谷","ok":0,"note":"Releases the exterior and moves qi strongly. There is no exterior pattern, and strong movement disperses fluids that are already short. 解表行氣力強。本案無表證，且強行則散其已虧之津。"},{"id":"ST40","t":"F&#275;ngl&#243;ng ST40 豐隆","ok":0,"note":"Transforms phlegm, and it is drying by nature. Yesterday's point for yesterday's patient — the last thing a dry man needs. 化痰之穴，其性主燥。昨日之穴為昨日之人而設，於燥者尤忌。"},{"id":"GB20","t":"F&#275;ngch&#237; GB20 風池","ok":0,"note":"Expels wind and subdues rising yang — reasonable if his yang were rising with headache and dizziness, but he has neither. Reach for it in chapter six's pattern, not this one. 祛風、平肝潛陽。若其陽亢而頭痛眩暈則可取，本案二者皆無。此第六章之證，非本案。"}],"tech":[{"t":"Reinforcing method, gentle and shallow, no moxa","c":"補法，宜輕宜淺，不施灸","ok":1,"note":"<b>Correct, and the moxa clause is why this case sits beside chapter ten.</b> You needled KI3 in the ferryman's winter and moxaed it generously — because that Kidney was cold and empty. This Kidney is hot and empty. Same point, same organ, opposite half: moxa warms and dries, and this man is already dry and running warm in the afternoons. Emptiness never automatically means warm it. 是，而不灸一條，正本案所以與第十章相對。吳伯之冬亦針太谿而重灸之，以其腎虛而寒。今其腎虛而熱。穴同、臟同、其半相反：灸性溫燥，而其人已燥且午後有熱。虛者未必宜溫。"},{"t":"Reinforcing method with generous moxa, as in chapter ten","c":"補法，重灸，如第十章","ok":0,"note":"The instinct to tonify is right and the moxa is the trap. Repeating a technique because the point is the same is how a student learns the map instead of the reasoning. 欲補之念不誤，而灸則其陷。以穴同而襲其法，是記其圖而未得其理。"},{"t":"Reducing method, to clear the heat","c":"瀉法，以清其熱","ok":0,"note":"The heat is real but it is empty heat. Draining it removes what little substance he has left, and the heat returns worse. 熱雖實有，然為虛熱。瀉之則奪其僅存之陰，熱反更甚。"},{"t":"Drain the Liver points and reinforce the Kidney points","c":"肝穴用瀉，腎穴用補","ok":0,"note":"That was chapter eleven's answer, where the Liver was full. Here the Liver is empty too — there is nothing to drain in this patient. 此第十一章之法，彼肝為實。今肝亦虛，通案無可瀉者。"}]};
